Whats working : 

The work described in the MuJoCo force-control README establishes a simulated
Franka Panda environment with joint position and velocity sensors and a
joint-space PD controller. It provides actuator force feedback and checks for
commanded and measured joint torques against configured actuator limits. An
extended Kalman filter and a sliding-mode observer estimate joint-space
disturbance torques from the simulated dynamics. A simulated six-axis load cell
and Cartesian force controller provide end-effector wrench sensing and
feedback. Hybrid position/force, cascaded force, impedance, and admittance
controllers, together with regression tests, complete the current simulation
foundation.

This foundation can be extended with a peg, hole, contact model, and
independently measured insertion success criteria to create a repeatable test
environment. The existing sensors and controllers can support contact
experiments and classical baselines, while their limits and simulated
load-cell behavior must be validated before real-world use. A fixed pi0.5
vision-language-action model is planned to supply nominal actions, with a
smaller residual policy providing bounded corrections. Four residual-policy
variants will separately enable or disable force observations and force-aware
reward shaping, while holding the backbone, action interface, safety checks,
and evaluation conditions consistent. An extended goal could be that after simulation validation and
independent hardware safety checks, randomized real-robot trials can compare
success rates, force-related outcomes, and the interaction between the two
factors.

# Research Outline: Force-Aware Residual Policy for Peg-in-Hole Insertion

## 1. Research Question

Does force information in the residual policy's observations and force-aware
reward shaping independently improve real-world peg-in-hole success, and do
they interact, when the same pretrained pi0.5 vision-language-action (VLA)
model provides the nominal action?

## 2. Motivation and Hypothesis

Peg insertion requires visual alignment, compliant contact, and controlled
motion after visual feedback becomes insufficient. A fixed pi0.5 policy
provides a consistent nominal action; a smaller learned residual policy
corrects that action. The study isolates whether (a) force observation,
(b) force-aware reward shaping, or (c) their combination improves insertion
performance.

Primary hypotheses:

1. Force observations improve robustness during contact and insertion.
2. A task-progress reward with a force penalty improves insertion without
   encouraging the policy to avoid necessary contact.
3. Using both produces a measurable interaction beyond either factor alone.

## 3. Task and Success Criteria

Use an 8 mm diameter peg with a specified 0.08 mm radial peg-hole clearance.
The target insertion depth is 25 mm. A trial meets the depth criterion when
its measured insertion depth is between 20 mm and 25 mm inclusive, and the
lateral criterion when the measured center offset is no more than 0.08 mm.
Define success independently of the learned reward and require both criteria,
a specified seated dwell time, and no predefined hard force-limit violation
or damage. Record each condition separately.

With a nominal 8.16 mm hole diameter, 0.08 mm is the ideal center-offset
clearance for a rigid, aligned 8 mm peg; tilt and manufacturing tolerances can
reduce the practical margin. Before implementing the evaluator, specify the
lateral measurement reference and frame, and verify that a success
measurement cannot count a peg beside the hole as inserted. Confirm the
depicted dimensions and depth direction, and specify how insertion and seated
dwell time will be independently measured.

## 4. Experimental Factors and Ablation

Keep the pi0.5 checkpoint, robot/action interface, demonstration data, training
budget, controller limits, and evaluation distribution fixed. Train/evaluate
the four residual-policy variants:

| Variant | Force in residual observation | Force-aware reward |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline | Off | Off |
| Observation only | On | Off |
| Reward only | Off | On |
| Combined | On | On |

Use multiple training seeds. Randomize peg/hole pose, initial alignment,
lighting, and other relevant trial conditions consistently across variants.
Use paired trials where practical.

## 5. Policy, Observation, and Action Interface

1. Verify the selected pi0.5 checkpoint, supported observation/action schema,
   license, inference hardware, and fine-tuning path from the official OpenPI
   documentation before committing to the integration.
2. Define a timestamped observation contract: camera image(s), joint position
   and velocity, gripper state, end-effector pose, and (for force-on variants)
   calibrated six-axis wrist force/torque with frame, units, filtering, and
   saturation status.
3. Define the residual action in a bounded, documented space (for example,
   Cartesian pose delta or twist), its frame, scale, update rate, and horizon.
   Combine it with pi0.5's nominal action through a deterministic adapter.
4. Preserve identical non-force observations and actions across ablations.
   When force is off, do not leak force readings through other features.
5. Normalize using training data only and save transforms/statistics with
   checkpoint and experiment metadata.

The existing MuJoCo implementation provides joint sensors, actuator force
feedback, torque observers, a six-axis end-effector load cell, joint PD control,
Cartesian wrench control, hybrid position/force control, cascaded force
control, impedance/admittance controllers, and regression tests. Reuse these
as simulation infrastructure, not as evidence that pi0.5 is already integrated
or that the simulated load cell equals a physical sensor.

## 6. Reward and Safety

Define reward terms before training. Use task progress (alignment and insertion
depth) and terminal success; in force-aware variants add a calibrated contact
quality/force term and penalties for excessive force, impacts, unstable
contact, and wasted motion. Do not reward simply minimizing force, because
insertion requires contact. Keep terminal success and safety evaluation
independent of the shaped reward.

Enforce hard force, torque, workspace, speed, and emergency-stop limits in the
low-level controller for every variant. Learned policy outputs must be
validated, bounded, and stopped on stale observations, sensor faults, or
communication loss. Do not rely on reward shaping for safety.

## 7. Staged Implementation Plan

### Stage A: Validate task definitions and protocol

Status: protocol draft; not yet approved for training or real-robot contact.
The values marked TBD must be decided and recorded before the corresponding
experiment begins. Existing MuJoCo controller limits are not an independent
real-robot safety system.

#### A1. Task geometry and evaluator

- Nominal peg diameter: 8 mm; stated radial clearance: 0.08 mm; resulting
  nominal hole diameter: 8.16 mm. Measure the physical parts and document
  diameter, roundness, straightness, and manufacturing tolerances.
- Target insertion depth: 25 mm; success depth range: 20-25 mm inclusive;
  maximum lateral center offset: 0.08 mm. Provisional simulation values are
  0.1 s continuous seating dwell and 0.2 degrees maximum tilt; physical values
  remain TBD.
- Define a hole-fixed frame with its origin at the hole entrance and its axial
  direction pointing into the hole. Measure lateral offset between the peg
  and hole axes at the entrance plane, and define the peg reference point and
  axial insertion depth in this frame before evaluation.
- Record depth, lateral offset, angular alignment, contact/safety outcome,
  and seating duration separately. Success requires actual insertion to the
  agreed depth, lateral error <= 0.08 mm, and the agreed seating/safety
  conditions; position alone must not count an adjacent peg as inserted.
- Specify independent measurement methods and uncertainty for both simulator
  and physical evaluation. In particular, demonstrate that the real measurement
  system can resolve 0.08 mm lateral error; otherwise report uncertainty and
  do not claim a definitive pass near the boundary.

#### A2. Hardware and calibration record

| Component | Simulator starting point | Real-robot specification |
|---|---|---|
| Robot | Existing MuJoCo Franka Panda | Model and controller: TBD |
| Gripper and peg | Panda gripper holds a free 8 mm diameter, 60 mm long peg through simulated finger contacts; trials start with the peg grasped | Gripper, grasp, peg material and dimensions: TBD |
| Hole and fixture | Kinematic fixture with 64 faceted walls approximating an 8.16 mm diameter, 30 mm deep hole; collidable table | Geometry, material, mounting, and compliance: TBD |
| Cameras | Two fixed workspace cameras, `task_left_camera` and `task_right_camera`, aimed at the fixture; one provisional hand-mounted `task_wrist_camera` | Real hardware TBD; camera model, placement, calibration, resolution and rate to be specified |
| Force/torque | Simulated six-axis hand load cell | Sensor, frame, range, sampling rate and limits: TBD |

- Record frame transforms among robot base, tool, load cell, camera(s), hole,
  and peg; calibrate and independently verify each transform.
- The initial protocol targets the MuJoCo Panda simulation; real-robot
  hardware selection and contact trials are deferred. The simulated left and
  right camera positions are defined and aimed at the fixture; verify their
  fields of view, feeds, timestamps, and image calibration for the intended
  data collection configuration.
- Characterize force sensor zero/bias, payload and gravity effects, sign
  convention, saturation, noise, latency, and synchronization. The MuJoCo
  hand sensor measures a transmitted wrench, not automatically a pure contact
  force; validate the estimate used for analysis and hardware protection.
- Establish measurement uncertainty for peg pose, depth, contact force, and
  timing. Recheck calibration and sensor zero at the prescribed intervals
  (intervals TBD).

#### A3. Trial procedure and randomization

1. Inspect fixture, peg, gripper, and safety systems; record calibration,
   hardware/configuration versions, and the test conditions.
2. Reset to a documented initial pose and verify grasp, sensor status, and
   valid observations. Randomize the starting peg/hole pose and lighting
   within predefined, physically reachable ranges (ranges TBD).
3. Assign the policy variant and initial condition using a recorded random
   seed; use matched trial conditions across variants where feasible.
4. Execute the approach and insertion until independently verified success,
   an operator/safety abort, or the episode time limit (time limit TBD).
   Log every attempt, including early aborts and invalid trials.
5. Record final pose, depth, lateral error, forces, retries, outcome, and
   reason for termination; inspect/reset the hardware before the next trial.
   Define excluded-trial rules and whether/how a safety abort ends a batch
   before data collection begins.

#### A4. Safety and stopping rules

- Set and validate task-specific hard axial/lateral force, moment, joint
  torque, speed, workspace, and episode-duration limits before real contact
  (all numeric thresholds TBD). Stop on limit violation, emergency stop,
  loss of communication, stale/invalid sensor data, unexpected fixture
  movement, dropped peg, or operator intervention.
- Define Fref for contact analysis separately from the hard safety threshold.
  Specify contact detection, filtering, sampling, force frame/sign, handling
  of missing samples, and the rule for overlapping force failure labels.
- Keep independent hardware protections active in every ablation, including
  force-observation-off variants. Validate emergency stop, fault handling,
  and safe recovery without assuming the learning policy will obey limits.

#### A5. Trial data contract

- Trial metadata: experiment/run ID, variant, policy checkpoint, training
  seed, trial seed, simulator/robot and software versions, calibration ID,
  geometry, randomized initial conditions, and time base.
- Time-stamped samples: camera frames or stable references, robot joint
  positions/velocities, gripper state, peg/hole pose and uncertainty, nominal
  and residual actions, executed action, wrench and sensor status, safety
  events, contact state, and task phase. Record sample rates and clock
  synchronization; log raw and processed force separately.
- Outcome record: success and its individual checks, final depth/lateral error,
  dwell duration, time to completion, peak contact force, intervals above Fref,
  retries, both failure labels where applicable, termination reason, and
  invalid/excluded-trial reason.
- Log force for analysis and independent safety across all variants, but
  prevent it from entering residual observations in force-off variants.
  Define data storage, access, retention, and schema version before collection.

Stage A exit: approve the unresolved criteria, hardware specification,
calibration/measurement procedure, randomization, stopping limits, and data
schema before training; separately pass hardware safety checks before real
contact.

### Stage B: Establish the simulator benchmark

The initial scaffold includes a Panda scene with a free peg, finger grasp,
table, faceted hole, two workspace cameras, and seeded fixture offsets.
`PegEpisode` now checks insertion, 0.1 s continuous seating, 0.2 degree tilt,
clearance along the inserted peg, force, drop, workspace, invalid state and
timeout. Intentional release is explicit. Contact-force samples, peak and
intervals above the reference are recorded through termination.

Preliminary simulation-only defaults are Fref = 1 N, a 5 N force stop, and
20 s timeout. Scripted kinematic fixture probes at 0.01 and 0.02 m/s measured
aligned peaks of 0.287 and 0 N, jam peaks of 10.645 and 15.583 N, and pressing
peaks of 5.720 and 6.268 N, respectively. Raw traces and the exact protocol
are in `mujoco_force_control/results/force_probes/` and the project README.
These few trials are not statistical validation or hardware safety limits.
Contact and grasp parameters are provisional; reset starts with the peg
between the fingers rather than executing a pick-up.

The Stage B trial starts with an already-grasped peg, checks initial contact
with both fingers, and keeps the fixture fixed while the robot inserts.
Robot-driven position, hybrid position/force, and Cartesian impedance
baselines now run on paired seeded fixture offsets and small fixture tilts.
The benchmark supplies reproducible Gaussian wrist-wrench noise and fixed
latency; camera and encoder observations remain ideal. It records termination
reasons, force summaries and heuristic failure labels, with optional per-step
traces. Labels deliberately leave ambiguous final geometry unclassified.
See the README for settings, commands, classification rules and limitations.
On the ten paired held-out seeds 10-19, position achieved 8 successes, hybrid
10, and impedance 9; failures and earlier untuned pilot outcomes are retained.
A five-seed wider-pose stress run produced 4, 5, and 5 successes respectively.
One position trial peaked at 44.9 N despite the 5 N stop because stopping occurs
after a physics step. These small, oracle-aligned simulator samples do not
validate hardware safety or establish comparative performance.

- Extend the existing MuJoCo Panda environment with a peg, hole, fixture,
  contact/friction parameters, randomized initial pose, and observable success
  metrics.
- Validate collision geometry and insertion tolerances against the clarified
  task dimensions.
- Add reproducible task reset, sensor noise/latency models, evaluation, and
  regression tests for success, failure, force limits, and reset behavior.
- Establish classical position, hybrid force, and impedance/admittance
  baselines before training a learned residual.

### Stage C: Integrate pi0.5 and collect demonstrations

- In progress: camera/state preprocessing, a non-actuating preview, and
  guarded simulation-only closed-loop execution are implemented and tested
  with the real pi05_droid checkpoint. The current configuration returned a
  15x8 chunk, despite the example describing 10x8. A recorded contact trial
  dropped the peg; it was not a successful insertion. The loop freezes physics
  during inference and is not a real-time or hardware-safe controller.
- A five-trial classical simulation pilot and a separate pi0.5 failure are
  recorded with disjoint classical split seeds. The pilot is too small to
  establish performance or train a residual; classical actuator controls
  are not DROID actions. Weight-specific license terms, a representative
  dataset, action conversion, and complete normalization remain unresolved.
  See the project README for the data contract and limitations.
- The v2 recorder additionally captures every physics-step input and replay
  state, linked to approximately 15 Hz camera samples. Direct conversion of
  observed joint motion to DROID-like commands and a bounded replay-fitted
  alternative were tested; both have substantial peg-tip reconstruction
  errors relative to 0.08 mm clearance. Do not train the planned imitation
  baseline until conversion and continuous held-out replay are validated.
- Implement and test camera/state preprocessing and action postprocessing.
- Verify inference timing and safe low-level action execution on the intended
  robot without contact, then in guarded low-risk contact trials.
- Collect task demonstrations and failures with synchronized images, state,
  wrench, action, phase, and outcome labels.
- Split data by trial/scene to avoid leakage; fit normalization on training data.

### Stage D: Train the residual policy and ablations

- Implement the residual policy over the fixed pi0.5 nominal action.
- Train all four 2x2 variants with matched data, compute budget, and seeds.
- Version code, model checkpoints, configs, normalization, reward weights, and
  random seeds for every run.
- Select checkpoints using a fixed validation protocol; reserve real-world test
  trials for final evaluation.

### Stage E: Evaluate in simulation and on the real robot

- Evaluate the four variants plus nominal-only and classical baselines in
  simulation, using paired randomized scenarios.
- Progress to real trials only after safety checks and predefined simulator
  criteria pass.
- Run randomized, paired, blinded-to-evaluator real-robot trials when feasible.
- Stop on force/torque limits, abnormal contact, dropped peg, fixture movement,
  sensor faults, or emergency-stop conditions.
- Report trial counts, exclusions, seeds, and all failures.

### Stage F: Analyze and report

- Primary outcome: independently adjudicated insertion success rate.
- Secondary outcomes: lateral error, depth error, peak and integrated force,
  force-limit events, completion time, retries, drops, and damage/failure events.
- Compute peak contact force and intervals continuously above Fref, from
  episode start through task completion for successful trials and through the
  episode end for failed trials. Distinguish Fref from the hard safety limit.
- Classify each failure independently by geometric and force mode, and report
  their counts by variant:
  - Geometric mode (tool's final location): entry failure if aligned over the
    hole but not entered; surface miss if still on the top surface without
    alignment; drift-off if it has left the top surface.
  - Force mode (contact while the held part is on the mating part's top
    surface): excessive pressing if force stays above Fref for at least 1 s
    continuously; unstable contact if surface contact lasts less than 1 s or
    lateral force exceeds axial force; stalled otherwise.
- Specify Fref, contact detection, force component frame and magnitude
  conventions, filtering, and sampling before analysis. Since excessive
  pressing and unstable contact can overlap, predefine whether to report
  multiple labels or use a precedence rule before plotting exclusive bars.
- Estimate observation main effect, reward main effect, and their interaction
  with confidence intervals; account for seed and trial/pose variability.
- Compare against nominal-only and classical baselines; report safety events,
  not only successful trials.
- State limitations and separate simulation findings from real-world claims.

## 8. Reproducibility and Deliverables

- Versioned task specification and success evaluator.
- Simulator configuration and randomized test suite.
- pi0.5 adapter, residual-policy code, and four ablation configurations.
- Demonstration/data schema, collection protocol, and calibrated sensor metadata.
- Training and evaluation scripts, seeds, checkpoints, and normalization stats.
- Trial-level results, analysis notebook/script, plots, and final report.

## 9. Decision Gates

1. Do not train until task geometry and success thresholds are unambiguous.
2. Do not run real contact trials until sensing, frame transforms, action limits,
   and independent safety stops pass bench tests.
3. Do not claim real-world improvement without held-out, randomized real-robot
   trials and uncertainty estimates.
4. Treat the force-on pi0.5 observation path as unverified until the chosen
   model interface and fine-tuning method are demonstrated end to end.